{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Pierre","familyName":"Magnot","name":"Magnot, Jean-Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contextuality, Holonomy and Discrete Fiber Bundles in Group-Valued Boltzmann Machines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T07 (primary), 22E70, 81P13, 57R22, 60B20, 68T05, 15B52","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T15:07:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a geometric extension of restricted Boltzmann machines (RBMs) by allowing weights to take values in abstract groups such as \\( \\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbb{R}) \\), \\( \\mathrm{SU}(2) \\), or even infinite-dimensional operator groups. This generalization enables the modeling of complex relational structures, including projective transformations, spinor dynamics, and functional symmetries, with direct applications to vision, language, and quantum learning.\n A central contribution of this work is the introduction of a \\emph{contextuality index} based on group-valued holonomies computed along cycles in the RBM graph. This index quantifies the global inconsistency or \"curvature\" induced by local weights, generalizing classical notions of coherence, consistency, and geometric flatness. We establish links with sheaf-theoretic contextuality, gauge theory, and noncommutative geometry, and provide numerical and diagrammatic examples in both finite and infinite dimensions.\n This framework opens novel directions in AI, from curvature-aware learning architectures to topological regularization in uncertain or adversarial environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:41:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10555","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10555","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10555","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandra","familyName":"Perez","name":"Perez, Alejandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chinedu","familyName":"Nwoye","name":"Nwoye, Chinedu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramtin Raji","familyName":"Kermani","name":"Kermani, Ramtin Raji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omid","familyName":"Mohareri","name":"Mohareri, Omid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Abdullah","familyName":"Jamal","name":"Jamal, Muhammad Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SurgLaVi: Large-Scale Hierarchical Dataset for Surgical Vision-Language Representation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T21:21:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:02:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:16:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language pre-training (VLP) offers unique advantages for surgery by aligning language with surgical videos, enabling workflow understanding and transfer across tasks without relying on expert-labeled datasets. However, progress in surgical VLP remains constrained by the limited scale, procedural diversity, semantic quality, and hierarchical structure of existing datasets. In this work, we present SurgLaVi, the largest and most diverse surgical vision-language dataset to date, comprising nearly 240k clip-caption pairs from more than 200 procedures, and featuring hierarchical levels at coarse-, mid-, and fine-level. At the core of SurgLaVi lies a fully automated pipeline that systematically generates fine-grained transcriptions of surgical videos and segments them into coherent procedural units. To ensure high-quality annotations, it applies dual-modality filtering to remove irrelevant and noisy samples. Within this framework, the resulting captions are enriched with contextual detail, producing annotations that are both semantically rich and easy to interpret. To ensure accessibility, we release SurgLaVi-$\\b{eta}$, an open-source derivative of 113k clip-caption pairs constructed entirely from public data, which is over four times larger than existing surgical VLP datasets. To demonstrate the value of the SurgLaVi datasets, we introduce SurgCLIP, a CLIP-style video-text contrastive framework with dual encoders, as a representative base model. SurgCLIP achieves consistent improvements across phase, step, action, and tool recognition, surpassing prior state-of-the-art methods, often by large margins. These results validate that large-scale, semantically rich, and hierarchically structured datasets directly translate into stronger and more generalizable representations, establishing SurgLaVi as a key resource for developing surgical foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10555","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:20:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Vasilkovsky","name":"Vasilkovsky, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Shakhrai","name":"Shakhrai, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiye","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Peiye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Tulyakov","name":"Tulyakov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Wonka","name":"Wonka, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Ying","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hsin-Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"T2Bs: Text-to-Character Blendshapes via Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T21:04:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present T2Bs, a framework for generating high-quality, animatable character head morphable models from text by combining static text-to-3D generation with video diffusion. Text-to-3D models produce detailed static geometry but lack motion synthesis, while video diffusion models generate motion with temporal and multi-view geometric inconsistencies. T2Bs bridges this gap by leveraging deformable 3D Gaussian splatting to align static 3D assets with video outputs. By constraining motion with static geometry and employing a view-dependent deformation MLP, T2Bs (i) outperforms existing 4D generation methods in accuracy and expressiveness while reducing video artifacts and view inconsistencies, and (ii) reconstructs smooth, coherent, fully registered 3D geometries designed to scale for building morphable models with diverse, realistic facial motions. This enables synthesizing expressive, animatable character heads that surpass current 4D generation techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:10:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10683","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10683","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10683","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felicia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Felicia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay J.","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Jay J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farzad","familyName":"Khalvati","name":"Khalvati, Farzad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Comparison and Evaluation of Fine-tuned Convolutional Neural Networks to Large Language Models for Image Classification and Segmentation of Brain Tumors on MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:26:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown strong performance in text-based healthcare tasks. However, their utility in image-based applications remains unexplored. We investigate the effectiveness of LLMs for medical imaging tasks, specifically glioma classification and segmentation, and compare their performance to that of traditional convolutional neural networks (CNNs). Using the BraTS 2020 dataset of multi-modal brain MRIs, we evaluated a general-purpose vision-language LLM (LLaMA 3.2 Instruct) both before and after fine-tuning, and benchmarked its performance against custom 3D CNNs. For glioma classification (Low-Grade vs. High-Grade), the CNN achieved 80% accuracy and balanced precision and recall. The general LLM reached 76% accuracy but suffered from a specificity of only 18%, often misclassifying Low-Grade tumors. Fine-tuning improved specificity to 55%, but overall performance declined (e.g., accuracy dropped to 72%). For segmentation, three methods - center point, bounding box, and polygon extraction, were implemented. CNNs accurately localized gliomas, though small tumors were sometimes missed. In contrast, LLMs consistently clustered predictions near the image center, with no distinction of glioma size, location, or placement. Fine-tuning improved output formatting but failed to meaningfully enhance spatial accuracy. The bounding polygon method yielded random, unstructured outputs. Overall, CNNs outperformed LLMs in both tasks. LLMs showed limited spatial understanding and minimal improvement from fine-tuning, indicating that, in their current form, they are not well-suited for image-based tasks. More rigorous fine-tuning or alternative training strategies may be needed for LLMs to achieve better performance, robustness, and utility in the medical space."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10683","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-Han","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chun-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendra","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable Part Diffusion 4D: Multi-View RGB and Kinematic Parts Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T23:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Stable Part Diffusion 4D (SP4D), a framework for generating paired RGB and kinematic part videos from monocular inputs. Unlike conventional part segmentation methods that rely on appearance-based semantic cues, SP4D learns to produce kinematic parts - structural components aligned with object articulation and consistent across views and time. SP4D adopts a dual-branch diffusion model that jointly synthesizes RGB frames and corresponding part segmentation maps. To simplify the architecture and flexibly enable different part counts, we introduce a spatial color encoding scheme that maps part masks to continuous RGB-like images. This encoding allows the segmentation branch to share the latent VAE from the RGB branch, while enabling part segmentation to be recovered via straightforward post-processing. A Bidirectional Diffusion Fusion (BiDiFuse) module enhances cross-branch consistency, supported by a contrastive part consistency loss to promote spatial and temporal alignment of part predictions. We demonstrate that the generated 2D part maps can be lifted to 3D to derive skeletal structures and harmonic skinning weights with few manual adjustments. To train and evaluate SP4D, we construct KinematicParts20K, a curated dataset of over 20K rigged objects selected and processed from Objaverse XL (Deitke et al., 2023), each paired with multi-view RGB and part video sequences. Experiments show that SP4D generalizes strongly to diverse scenarios, including real-world videos, novel generated objects, and rare articulated poses, producing kinematic-aware outputs suitable for downstream animation and motion-related tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Page: https://stablepartdiffusion4d.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hootan","familyName":"Nakhost","name":"Nakhost, Hootan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajarishi","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Rajarishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan Ö.","familyName":"Arık","name":"Arık, Sercan Ö.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:45:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models, while offering immense creative potential, are highly reliant on human intervention, posing significant usability challenges that often necessitate manual, iterative prompt engineering over often underspecified prompts. This paper introduces Maestro, a novel self-evolving image generation system that enables T2I models to autonomously self-improve generated images through iterative evolution of prompts, using only an initial prompt. Maestro incorporates two key innovations: 1) self-critique, where specialized multimodal LLM (MLLM) agents act as 'critics' to identify weaknesses in generated images, correct for under-specification, and provide interpretable edit signals, which are then integrated by a 'verifier' agent while preserving user intent; and 2) self-evolution, utilizing MLLM-as-a-judge for head-to-head comparisons between iteratively generated images, eschewing problematic images, and evolving creative prompt candidates that align with user intents. Extensive experiments on complex T2I tasks using black-box models demonstrate that Maestro significantly improves image quality over initial prompts and state-of-the-art automated methods, with effectiveness scaling with more advanced MLLM components. This work presents a robust, interpretable, and effective pathway towards self-improving T2I generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables (22 pages, 9 figures and 3 tables including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Abdoli","name":"Abdoli, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rudi","familyName":"Cilibrasi","name":"Cilibrasi, Rudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rima","familyName":"Al-Shikh","name":"Al-Shikh, Rima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding AI Evaluation Patterns: How Different GPT Models Assess Vision-Language Descriptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T14:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:51:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As AI systems increasingly evaluate other AI outputs, understanding their assessment behavior becomes crucial for preventing cascading biases. This study analyzes vision-language descriptions generated by NVIDIA's Describe Anything Model and evaluated by three GPT variants (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-5) to uncover distinct \"evaluation personalities\" the underlying assessment strategies and biases each model demonstrates. GPT-4o-mini exhibits systematic consistency with minimal variance, GPT-4o excels at error detection, while GPT-5 shows extreme conservatism with high variability. Controlled experiments using Gemini 2.5 Pro as an independent question generator validate that these personalities are inherent model properties rather than artifacts. Cross-family analysis through semantic similarity of generated questions reveals significant divergence: GPT models cluster together with high similarity while Gemini exhibits markedly different evaluation strategies. All GPT models demonstrate a consistent 2:1 bias favoring negative assessment over positive confirmation, though this pattern appears family-specific rather than universal across AI architectures. These findings suggest that evaluation competence does not scale with general capability and that robust AI assessment requires diverse architectural perspectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:48:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Mayer","name":"Mayer, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shohei","familyName":"Noguchi","name":"Noguchi, Shohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Berestov","name":"Berestov, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiro","familyName":"Takatori","name":"Takatori, Jiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language-based Color ISP Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:37:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a method for tuning the parameters of a color adjustment Image Signal Processor (ISP) algorithmic \"block\" using language prompts. This enables the user to impart a particular visual style to the ISP-processed image simply by describing it through a text prompt. To do this, we first implement the ISP block in a differentiable manner. Then, we define an objective function using an off-the-shelf, pretrained vision-language model (VLM) such that the objective is minimized when the ISP processed image is most visually similar to the input language prompt. Finally, we optimize the ISP parameters using gradient descent. Experimental results demonstrate tuning of ISP parameters with different language prompts, and compare the performance of different pretrained VLMs and optimization strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Color and Imaging Conference (CIC) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Bărbălau","name":"Bărbălau, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian Daniel","familyName":"Păduraru","name":"Păduraru, Cristian Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teodor","familyName":"Poncu","name":"Poncu, Teodor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Tifrea","name":"Tifrea, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Burceanu","name":"Burceanu, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Sparse Autoencoders: Select-and-Project for Fairness and Control from Encoder Features Alone"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T06:36:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:17:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T13:23:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely employed for mechanistic interpretability and model steering. Within this context, steering is by design performed by means of decoding altered SAE intermediate representations. This procedure essentially rewrites the original activations as a weighted sum of decoder features. In contrast to existing literature, we forward an encoder-centric alternative to model steering which demonstrates a stronger cross-modal performance. We introduce S\u0026amp;P Top-K, a retraining-free and computationally lightweight Selection and Projection framework that identifies Top-K encoder features aligned with a sensitive attribute or behavior, optionally aggregates them into a single control axis, and computes an orthogonal projection to be subsequently applied directly in the model's native embedding space. In vision-language models, it improves fairness metrics on CelebA and FairFace by up to 3.2 times over conventional SAE usage, and in large language models, it substantially reduces aggressiveness and sycophancy in Llama-3 8B Instruct, achieving up to 3.6 times gains over masked reconstruction. These findings suggest that encoder-centric interventions provide a general, efficient, and more effective mechanism for shaping model behavior at inference time than the traditional decoder-centric use of SAEs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunlei","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Kunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihua","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenUrban3D: Annotation-Free Open-Vocabulary Semantic Segmentation of Large-Scale Urban Point Clouds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-vocabulary semantic segmentation enables models to recognize and segment objects from arbitrary natural language descriptions, offering the flexibility to handle novel, fine-grained, or functionally defined categories beyond fixed label sets. While this capability is crucial for large-scale urban point clouds that support applications such as digital twins, smart city management, and urban analytics, it remains largely unexplored in this domain. The main obstacles are the frequent absence of high-quality, well-aligned multi-view imagery in large-scale urban point cloud datasets and the poor generalization of existing three-dimensional (3D) segmentation pipelines across diverse urban environments with substantial variation in geometry, scale, and appearance. To address these challenges, we present OpenUrban3D, the first 3D open-vocabulary semantic segmentation framework for large-scale urban scenes that operates without aligned multi-view images, pre-trained point cloud segmentation networks, or manual annotations. Our approach generates robust semantic features directly from raw point clouds through multi-view, multi-granularity rendering, mask-level vision-language feature extraction, and sample-balanced fusion, followed by distillation into a 3D backbone model. This design enables zero-shot segmentation for arbitrary text queries while capturing both semantic richness and geometric priors. Extensive experiments on large-scale urban benchmarks, including SensatUrban and SUM, show that OpenUrban3D achieves significant improvements in both segmentation accuracy and cross-scene generalization over existing methods, demonstrating its potential as a flexible and scalable solution for 3D urban scene understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huynh T. T.","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Huynh T. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Sander","name":"Sander, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achraf","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Achraf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Jalaian","name":"Jalaian, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathaniel D.","familyName":"Bastian","name":"Bastian, Nathaniel D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neurosymbolic AI Transfer Learning Improves Network Intrusion Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T15:12:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transfer learning is commonly utilized in various fields such as computer vision, natural language processing, and medical imaging due to its impressive capability to address subtasks and work with different datasets. However, its application in cybersecurity has not been thoroughly explored. In this paper, we present an innovative neurosymbolic AI framework designed for network intrusion detection systems, which play a crucial role in combating malicious activities in cybersecurity. Our framework leverages transfer learning and uncertainty quantification. The findings indicate that transfer learning models, trained on large and well-structured datasets, outperform neural-based models that rely on smaller datasets, paving the way for a new era in cybersecurity solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 2 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:48:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:48:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:48:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToMA: Token Merge with Attention for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-30T17:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel in high-fidelity image generation but face scalability limits due to transformers' quadratic attention complexity. Plug-and-play token reduction methods like ToMeSD and ToFu reduce FLOPs by merging redundant tokens in generated images but rely on GPU-inefficient operations (e.g., sorting, scattered writes), introducing overheads that negate theoretical speedups when paired with optimized attention implementations (e.g., FlashAttention). To bridge this gap, we propose Token Merge with Attention (ToMA), an off-the-shelf method that redesigns token reduction for GPU-aligned efficiency, with three key contributions: 1) a reformulation of token merge as a submodular optimization problem to select diverse tokens; 2) merge/unmerge as an attention-like linear transformation via GPU-friendly matrix operations; and 3) exploiting latent locality and sequential redundancy (pattern reuse) to minimize overhead. ToMA reduces SDXL/Flux generation latency by 24%/23%, respectively (with DINO $Δ\u0026lt; 0.07$), outperforming prior methods. This work bridges the gap between theoretical and practical efficiency for transformers in diffusion. Code available at https://github.com/WenboLuu/ToMA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025). Code available at https://github.com/wenboluu/ToMA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TrueSkin: Towards Fair and Accurate Skin Tone Recognition and Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T20:58:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T05:22:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:27:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Skin tone recognition and generation play important roles in model fairness, healthcare, and generative AI, yet they remain challenging due to the lack of comprehensive datasets and robust methodologies. Compared to other human image analysis tasks, state-of-the-art large multimodal models (LMMs) and image generation models struggle to recognize and synthesize skin tones accurately. To address this, we introduce TrueSkin, a dataset with 7299 images systematically categorized into 6 classes, collected under diverse lighting conditions, camera angles, and capture settings. Using TrueSkin, we benchmark existing recognition and generation approaches, revealing substantial biases: LMMs tend to misclassify intermediate skin tones as lighter ones, whereas generative models struggle to accurately produce specified skin tones when influenced by inherent biases from unrelated attributes in the prompts, such as hairstyle or environmental context. We further demonstrate that training a recognition model on TrueSkin improves classification accuracy by more than 20\\% compared to LMMs and conventional approaches, and fine-tuning with TrueSkin significantly improves skin tone fidelity in image generation models. Our findings highlight the need for comprehensive datasets like TrueSkin, which not only serves as a benchmark for evaluating existing models but also provides a valuable training resource to enhance fairness and accuracy in skin tone recognition and generation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The dataset is available for download at https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_ndw5uyY4h4DLL5iGTL4bVDKdE_g_H4B/view?usp=sharing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Daming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViScratch: Using Large Language Models and Gameplay Videos for Automated Feedback in Scratch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:12:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Block-based programming environments such as Scratch are increasingly popular in programming education, in particular for young learners. While the use of blocks helps prevent syntax errors, semantic bugs remain common and difficult to debug. Existing tools for Scratch debugging rely heavily on predefined rules or user manual inputs, and crucially, they ignore the platform's inherently visual nature.\n We introduce ViScratch, the first multimodal feedback generation system for Scratch that leverages both the project's block code and its generated gameplay video to diagnose and repair bugs. ViScratch uses a two-stage pipeline: a vision-language model first aligns visual symptoms with code structure to identify a single critical issue, then proposes minimal, abstract syntax tree level repairs that are verified via execution in the Scratch virtual machine.\n We evaluate ViScratch on a set of real-world Scratch projects against state-of-the-art LLM-based tools and human testers. Results show that gameplay video is a crucial debugging signal: ViScratch substantially outperforms prior tools in both bug identification and repair quality, even without access to project descriptions or goals. This work demonstrates that video can serve as a first-class specification in visual programming environments, opening new directions for LLM-based debugging beyond symbolic code alone."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehui","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Dehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The System Description of CPS Team for Track on Driving with Language of CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This report outlines our approach using vision language model systems for the Driving with Language track of the CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge. We have exclusively utilized the DriveLM-nuScenes dataset for training our models. Our systems are built on the LLaVA models, which we enhanced through fine-tuning with the LoRA and DoRA methods. Additionally, we have integrated depth information from open-source depth estimation models to enrich the training and inference processes. For inference, particularly with multiple-choice and yes/no questions, we adopted a Chain-of-Thought reasoning approach to improve the accuracy of the results. This comprehensive methodology enabled us to achieve a top score of 0.7799 on the validation set leaderboard, ranking 1st on the leaderboard."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Xiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hao-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PanoLora: Bridging Perspective and Panoramic Video Generation with LoRA Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:05:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating high-quality 360° panoramic videos remains a significant challenge due to the fundamental differences between panoramic and traditional perspective-view projections. While perspective videos rely on a single viewpoint with a limited field of view, panoramic content requires rendering the full surrounding environment, making it difficult for standard video generation models to adapt. Existing solutions often introduce complex architectures or large-scale training, leading to inefficiency and suboptimal results. Motivated by the success of Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) in style transfer tasks, we propose treating panoramic video generation as an adaptation problem from perspective views. Through theoretical analysis, we demonstrate that LoRA can effectively model the transformation between these projections when its rank exceeds the degrees of freedom in the task. Our approach efficiently fine-tunes a pretrained video diffusion model using only approximately 1,000 videos while achieving high-quality panoramic generation. Experimental results demonstrate that our method maintains proper projection geometry and surpasses previous state-of-the-art approaches in visual quality, left-right consistency, and motion diversity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yazhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuchi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiuchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Not Understanding: A Benchmark on Perception-Cognition Disparities in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T08:45:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models' ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding. The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty. Perception tasks evaluate the model's ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of nine leading MLLMs, including GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-pro, and GPT-4o, on EmoBench-Reddit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"I need to modify the content of the article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:35:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yunheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuetong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taowen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamNav: A Trajectory-Based Imaginative Framework for Zero-Shot Vision-and-Language Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:54:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-and-Language Navigation in Continuous Environments (VLN-CE), which links language instructions to perception and control in the real world, is a core capability of embodied robots. Recently, large-scale pretrained foundation models have been leveraged as shared priors for perception, reasoning, and action, enabling zero-shot VLN without task-specific training. However, existing zero-shot VLN methods depend on costly perception and passive scene understanding, collapsing control to point-level choices. As a result, they are expensive to deploy, misaligned in action semantics, and short-sighted in planning. To address these issues, we present DreamNav that focuses on the following three aspects: (1) for reducing sensory cost, our EgoView Corrector aligns viewpoints and stabilizes egocentric perception; (2) instead of point-level actions, our Trajectory Predictor favors global trajectory-level planning to better align with instruction semantics; and (3) to enable anticipatory and long-horizon planning, we propose an Imagination Predictor to endow the agent with proactive thinking capability. On VLN-CE and real-world tests, DreamNav sets a new zero-shot state-of-the-art (SOTA), outperforming the strongest egocentric baseline with extra information by up to 7.49\\% and 18.15\\% in terms of SR and SPL metrics. To our knowledge, this is the first zero-shot VLN method to unify trajectory-level planning and active imagination while using only egocentric inputs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:57:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Daiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Pingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Sliders: Mastering the Art of Diffusion-based Image Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T10:48:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:41:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the realm of image generation, the quest for realism and customization has never been more pressing. While existing methods like concept sliders have made strides, they often falter when it comes to no-AIGC images, particularly images captured in real world settings. To bridge this gap, we introduce Beyond Sliders, an innovative framework that integrates GANs and diffusion models to facilitate sophisticated image manipulation across diverse image categories. Improved upon concept sliders, our method refines the image through fine grained guidance both textual and visual in an adversarial manner, leading to a marked enhancement in image quality and realism. Extensive experimental validation confirms the robustness and versatility of Beyond Sliders across a spectrum of applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunfeng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Congxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Vision-Language Models by Self-Injecting Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:26:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:45:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) suffer from serious hallucination problems, where the model-generated responses are inconsistent with the visual inputs. Existing hallucination mitigation methods are mainly based on preference alignment and require external human annotations or auxiliary models for preference data collection, which increase costs and limit sustainable improvement. To tackle these challenges, we propose Autonomous Preference Alignment via Self-Injection (APASI), a novel and generalizable method that mitigates hallucinations without external dependencies. APASI leverages the target LVLM to self-inject hallucinations into a generated response, creating a pair of responses with varying preference levels. During the self-injection process, the dis-preferred response is generated based on three key observations of hallucinations, ensuring it simulates real hallucination patterns. This fidelity offers an accurate learning signal for hallucination mitigation. Moreover, APASI incorporates an iterative alignment training strategy combined with curriculum learning to periodically update the preference data with increasing challenge, enabling stable and continuous enhancement of the LVLM. Extensive experiments across six benchmarks show that APASI not only effectively mitigates hallucinations for three baseline models but also achieves comparable or even superior performance to alignment-based methods with external dependency, thereby demonstrating its effectiveness and generalization capability. The code is available at https://github.com/davidluciolu/APASI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"emnlp 2025 accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Ajoudani","name":"Ajoudani, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ActivePose: Active 6D Object Pose Estimation and Tracking for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:50:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T13:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:44:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 6-DoF object pose estimation and tracking are critical for reliable robotic manipulation. However, zero-shot methods often fail under viewpoint-induced ambiguities and fixed-camera setups struggle when objects move or become self-occluded. To address these challenges, we propose an active pose estimation pipeline that combines a Vision-Language Model (VLM) with \"robotic imagination\" to dynamically detect and resolve ambiguities in real time. In an offline stage, we render a dense set of views of the CAD model, compute the FoundationPose entropy for each view, and construct a geometric-aware prompt that includes low-entropy (unambiguous) and high-entropy (ambiguous) examples. At runtime, the system: (1) queries the VLM on the live image for an ambiguity score; (2) if ambiguity is detected, imagines a discrete set of candidate camera poses by rendering virtual views, scores each based on a weighted combination of VLM ambiguity probability and FoundationPose entropy, and then moves the camera to the Next-Best-View (NBV) to obtain a disambiguated pose estimation. Furthermore, since moving objects may leave the camera's field of view, we introduce an active pose tracking module: a diffusion-policy trained via imitation learning, which generates camera trajectories that preserve object visibility and minimize pose ambiguity. Experiments in simulation and real-world show that our approach significantly outperforms classical baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6D Pose, Diffusion Policy, Robot Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:58Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shresth","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Shresth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Gopalkrishnan","name":"Gopalkrishnan, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrik I.","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Henrik I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Generalization in Vision-Language-Action Models by Preserving Pretrained Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:08:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T02:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:18:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language-action (VLA) models finetuned from vision-language models (VLMs) hold the promise of leveraging rich pretrained representations to build generalist robots across diverse tasks and environments. However, direct fine-tuning on robot data often disrupts these representations and limits generalization. We present a framework that better preserves pretrained features while adapting them for robot manipulation. Our approach introduces three components: (i) a dual-encoder design with one frozen vision encoder to retain pretrained features and another trainable for task adaptation, (ii) a string-based action tokenizer that casts continuous actions into character sequences aligned with the model's pretraining domain, and (iii) a co-training strategy that combines robot demonstrations with vision-language datasets emphasizing spatial reasoning and affordances. Evaluations in simulation and on real robots show that our method improves robustness to visual perturbations, generalization to novel instructions and environments, and overall task success compared to baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://gen-vla.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Abaskohi","name":"Abaskohi, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CEMTM: Contextual Embedding-based Multimodal Topic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:07:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:55:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T16:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce CEMTM, a context-enhanced multimodal topic model designed to infer coherent and interpretable topic structures from both short and long documents containing text and images. CEMTM builds on fine-tuned large vision language models (LVLMs) to obtain contextualized embeddings, and employs a distributional attention mechanism to weight token-level contributions to topic inference. A reconstruction objective aligns topic-based representations with the document embedding, encouraging semantic consistency across modalities. Unlike existing approaches, CEMTM can process multiple images per document without repeated encoding and maintains interpretability through explicit word-topic and document-topic distributions. Extensive experiments on six multimodal benchmarks show that CEMTM consistently outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines, achieving a remarkable average LLM score of 2.61. Further analysis shows its effectiveness in downstream few-shot retrieval and its ability to capture visually grounded semantics in complex domains such as scientific articles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Taherin","name":"Taherin, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Juyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Platform Scaling of Vision-Language-Action Models from Edge to Cloud GPUs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T15:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as powerful generalist policies for robotic control, yet their performance scaling across model architectures and hardware platforms, as well as their associated power budgets, remain poorly understood. This work presents an evaluation of five representative VLA models -- spanning state-of-the-art baselines and two newly proposed architectures -- targeting edge and datacenter GPU platforms. Using the LIBERO benchmark, we measure accuracy alongside system-level metrics, including latency, throughput, and peak memory usage, under varying edge power constraints and high-performance datacenter GPU configurations. Our results identify distinct scaling trends: (1) architectural choices, such as action tokenization and model backbone size, strongly influence throughput and memory footprint; (2) power-constrained edge devices exhibit non-linear performance degradation, with some configurations matching or exceeding older datacenter GPUs; and (3) high-throughput variants can be achieved without significant accuracy loss. These findings provide actionable insights when selecting and optimizing VLAs across a range of deployment constraints. Our work challenges current assumptions about the superiority of datacenter hardware for robotic inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems, and Computers 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Kaliosis","name":"Kaliosis, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Rambow","name":"Rambow, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan E.","familyName":"Brennan","name":"Brennan, Susan E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LVLMs are Bad at Overhearing Human Referential Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T02:03:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:58:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:25:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"During spontaneous conversations, speakers collaborate on novel referring expressions, which they can then re-use in subsequent conversations. Understanding such referring expressions is an important ability for an embodied agent, so that it can carry out tasks in the real world. This requires integrating and understanding language, vision, and conversational interaction. We study the capabilities of seven state-of-the-art Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs) as overhearers to a corpus of spontaneous conversations between pairs of human discourse participants engaged in a collaborative object-matching task. We find that such a task remains challenging for current LVLMs and they all fail to show a consistent performance improvement as they overhear more conversations from the same discourse participants repeating the same task for multiple rounds. We release our corpus and code for reproducibility and to facilitate future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:11:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongxiao","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Tongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manni","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Manni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T03:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T01:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:19:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) grounding is a fundamental task for building GUI agents. However, general vision-language models (VLMs) struggle with this task due to a lack of specific optimization. We identify a key gap in this paper: while VLMs exhibit significant latent grounding potential, as demonstrated by their performance measured by Pointing Game, they underperform when tasked with outputting explicit coordinates. To address this discrepancy and bypass the high data and annotation costs of current fine-tuning approaches, we propose three zero-shot auxiliary reasoning methods. By providing explicit spatial cues such as axes, grids and labeled intersections as part of the input image, these methods enable VLMs to better articulate their implicit spatial understanding capabilities. We evaluate these methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks across seven open-source and proprietary VLMs. Experimental results show substantial gains from auxiliary reasoning. Mark-Grid Scaffold boosts Gemini-3.1-Pro from 11.72\\% under direct inference to 95.20\\% on ScreenSpot-v2, achieves state-of-the-art performance on ScreenSpot, and approaches the strongest fine-tuned methods on ScreenSpot-v2 and UI-I2E-Bench. Our code is available at https://github.com/liweim/AuxiliaryReasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyun","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MVQA-68K: A Multi-dimensional and Causally-annotated Dataset with Quality Interpretability for Video Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T05:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:03:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of video generation models such as Sora, video quality assessment (VQA) is becoming increasingly crucial for selecting high-quality videos from large-scale datasets used in pre-training. Traditional VQA methods, typically producing single numerical scores, often lack comprehensiveness and interpretability. To address these challenges, we introduce MVQA-68K, a novel multi-dimensional VQA dataset comprising over 68,000 carefully annotated videos, covering seven essential quality dimensions: overall aesthetics, camera movement, dynamic degree, texture detail, composition, visual quality, and factual consistency. Each annotation includes detailed chain-of-thought reasoning to facilitate interpretability and comprehensive understanding. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MVQA-68K significantly enhances the performance of various multimodal large language models (MLLMs) on the VQA task, achieving state-of-the-art results not only on our internal test set (Fig.1) but also on public benchmarks including LSVQ-test, LSVQ-1080p, and LIVE-VQC. Meantime, incorporating explicit reasoning process during VQA training substantially boosts the zero-shot generalization. Code and dataset will be available at github: https://github.com/Controller01-ai/MVQA-68K"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:13Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:06:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai-Man","familyName":"Po","name":"Po, Lai-Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hongzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangcheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yuzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AesBiasBench: Evaluating Bias and Alignment in Multimodal Language Models for Personalized Image Aesthetic Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:25:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:04:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are increasingly applied in Personalized Image Aesthetic Assessment (PIAA) as a scalable alternative to expert evaluations. However, their predictions may reflect subtle biases influenced by demographic factors such as gender, age, and education. In this work, we propose AesBiasBench, a benchmark designed to evaluate MLLMs along two complementary dimensions: (1) stereotype bias, quantified by measuring variations in aesthetic evaluations across demographic groups; and (2) alignment between model outputs and genuine human aesthetic preferences. Our benchmark covers three subtasks (Aesthetic Perception, Assessment, Empathy) and introduces structured metrics (IFD, NRD, AAS) to assess both bias and alignment. We evaluate 19 MLLMs, including proprietary models (e.g., GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet) and open-source models (e.g., InternVL-2.5, Qwen2.5-VL). Results indicate that smaller models exhibit stronger stereotype biases, whereas larger models align more closely with human preferences. Incorporating identity information often exacerbates bias, particularly in emotional judgments. These findings underscore the importance of identity-aware evaluation frameworks in subjective vision-language tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:06:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"HG","familyName":"Ranjani","name":"Ranjani, HG","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rutuja","familyName":"Prabhudesai","name":"Prabhudesai, Rutuja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring Visual Understanding in Telecom domain: Performance Metrics for Image-to-UML conversion using VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:08:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:08:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Telecom domain 3GPP documents are replete with images containing sequence diagrams. Advances in Vision-Language Large Models (VLMs) have eased conversion of such images to machine-readable PlantUML (puml) formats. However, there is a gap in evaluation of such conversions - existing works do not compare puml scripts for various components. In this work, we propose performance metrics to measure the effectiveness of such conversions. A dataset of sequence diagrams from 3GPP documents is chosen to be representative of domain-specific actual scenarios. We compare puml outputs from two VLMs - Claude Sonnet and GPT-4V - against manually created ground truth representations. We use version control tools to capture differences and introduce standard performance metrics to measure accuracies along various components: participant identification, message flow accuracy, sequence ordering, and grouping construct preservation. We demonstrate effectiveness of proposed metrics in quantifying conversion errors across various components of puml scripts. The results show that nodes, edges and messages are accurately captured. However, we observe that VLMs do not necessarily perform well on complex structures such as notes, box, groups. Our experiments and performance metrics indicates a need for better representation of these components in training data for fine-tuned VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:08:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11714","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11714","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11714","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hafza","familyName":"Eman","name":"Eman, Hafza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furqan","familyName":"Shaukat","name":"Shaukat, Furqan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Hamza","familyName":"Zafar","name":"Zafar, Muhammad Hamza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Muhammad","familyName":"Anwar","name":"Anwar, Syed Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EMeRALDS: Electronic Medical Record Driven Automated Lung Nodule Detection and Classification in Thoracic CT Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T09:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Objective: Lung cancer is a leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, primarily due to delayed diagnosis and poor early detection. This study aims to develop a computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) system that leverages large vision-language models (VLMs) for the accurate detection and classification of pulmonary nodules in computed tomography (CT) scans.\n Methods: We propose an end-to-end CAD pipeline consisting of two modules: (i) a detection module (CADe) based on the Segment Anything Model 2 (SAM2), in which the standard visual prompt is replaced with a text prompt encoded by CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining), and (ii) a diagnosis module (CADx) that calculates similarity scores between segmented nodules and radiomic features. To add clinical context, synthetic electronic medical records (EMRs) were generated using radiomic assessments by expert radiologists and combined with similarity scores for final classification. The method was tested on the publicly available LIDC-IDRI dataset (1,018 CT scans).\n Results: The proposed approach demonstrated strong performance in zero-shot lung nodule analysis. The CADe module achieved a Dice score of 0.92 and an IoU of 0.85 for nodule segmentation. The CADx module attained a specificity of 0.97 for malignancy classification, surpassing existing fully supervised methods.\n Conclusions: The integration of VLMs with radiomics and synthetic EMRs allows for accurate and clinically relevant CAD of pulmonary nodules in CT scans. The proposed system shows strong potential to enhance early lung cancer detection, increase diagnostic confidence, and improve patient management in routine clinical workflows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11714","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:09:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:09:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:09:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brae","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Brae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chalse","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chalse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ellie","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ellie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lights","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lights","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucy","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Lucy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Make","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Make","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shalfun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shalfun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Starrick","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Starrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sylas","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sylas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Igniting VLMs toward the Embodied Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:40:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While foundation models show remarkable progress in language and vision, existing vision-language models (VLMs) still have limited spatial and embodiment understanding. Transferring VLMs to embodied domains reveals fundamental mismatches between modalities, pretraining distributions, and training objectives, leaving action comprehension and generation as a central bottleneck on the path to AGI.\n We introduce WALL-OSS, an end-to-end embodied foundation model that leverages large-scale multimodal pretraining to achieve (1) embodiment-aware vision-language understanding, (2) strong language-action association, and (3) robust manipulation capability.\n Our approach employs a tightly coupled architecture and multi-strategies training curriculum that enables Unified Cross-Level CoT-seamlessly unifying instruction reasoning, subgoal decomposition, and fine-grained action synthesis within a single differentiable framework.\n Our results show that WALL-OSS attains high success on complex long-horizon manipulations, demonstrates strong instruction-following capabilities, complex understanding and reasoning, and outperforms strong baselines, thereby providing a reliable and scalable path from VLMs to embodied foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:10:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:10:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:10:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiduo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuwei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanwu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuanwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengju","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecVLM: Fast Speculative Decoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T03:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:45:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding is a powerful way to accelerate autoregressive large language models (LLMs), but directly porting it to vision-language models (VLMs) faces unique systems constraints: the prefill stage is dominated by visual tokens whose count scales with image resolution and video length, inflating both compute and memory, especially the key-value (KV) cache. We study speculative decoding for VLMs and introduce SpecVLM, a practical system that (1) establishes a strong EAGLE-2-style baseline, EagleVLM, delivering 1.5--2.3x end-to-end speedups over full autoregressive inference, and (2) further accelerates VLM inference with an elastic visual compressor that adaptively selects among pruning, pooling, convolution, and resampler primitives to balance FLOPs/parameters and accuracy per input. To avoid costly offline distillation corpora, we propose an online-logit distillation protocol that trains the draft model with on-the-fly teacher logits and penultimate features using a combined cross-entropy and Smooth L1 objective, eliminating storage and preprocessing while remaining compute-efficient. This protocol reveals a training-time scaling effect: longer online training monotonically increases the draft model's average accepted length, improving speculative efficiency. Empirically, SpecVLM achieves additional acceleration, culminating in 2.5--2.9x end-to-end speedups within 5 epochs across LLaVA and MMMU, consistently over resolutions and task difficulties, while preserving the target model's output distribution (lossless decoding). Our code is available at https://github.com/haiduo/SpecVLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:51Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11839","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11839","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11839","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimian","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zimian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cewu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Cewu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TrajBooster: Boosting Humanoid Whole-Body Manipulation via Trajectory-Centric Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:25:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:11:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T12:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:56:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Vision-Language-Action models show potential to generalize across embodiments but struggle to quickly align with a new robot's action space when high-quality demonstrations are scarce, especially for bipedal humanoids. We present TrajBooster, a cross-embodiment framework that leverages abundant wheeled-humanoid data to boost bipedal VLA. Our key idea is to use end-effector trajectories as a morphology-agnostic interface. TrajBooster (i) extracts 6D dual-arm end-effector trajectories from real-world wheeled humanoids, (ii) retargets them in simulation to Unitree G1 with a whole-body controller trained via a heuristic-enhanced harmonized online DAgger to lift low-dimensional trajectory references into feasible high-dimensional whole-body actions, and (iii) forms heterogeneous triplets that couple source vision/language with target humanoid-compatible actions to post-pre-train a VLA, followed by only 10 minutes of teleoperation data collection on the target humanoid domain. Deployed on Unitree G1, our policy achieves beyond-tabletop household tasks, enabling squatting, cross-height manipulation, and coordinated whole-body motion with markedly improved robustness and generalization. Results show that TrajBooster allows existing wheeled-humanoid data to efficiently strengthen bipedal humanoid VLA performance, reducing reliance on costly same-embodiment data while enhancing action space understanding and zero-shot skill transfer capabilities. For more details, For more details, please refer to our \\href{https://jiachengliu3.github.io/TrajBooster/}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11839","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:26Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:37:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Lebailly","name":"Lebailly, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Veerabadran","name":"Veerabadran, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satwik","familyName":"Kottur","name":"Kottur, Satwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Ridgeway","name":"Ridgeway, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Louis","familyName":"Iuzzolino","name":"Iuzzolino, Michael Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synthetic Captions for Open-Vocabulary Zero-Shot Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:26:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative vision-language models (VLMs) exhibit strong high-level image understanding but lack spatially dense alignment between vision and language modalities, as our findings indicate. Orthogonal to advancements in generative VLMs, another line of research has focused on representation learning for vision-language alignment, targeting zero-shot inference for dense tasks like segmentation. In this work, we bridge these two directions by densely aligning images with synthetic descriptions generated by VLMs. Synthetic captions are inexpensive, scalable, and easy to generate, making them an excellent source of high-level semantic understanding for dense alignment methods. Empirically, our approach outperforms prior work on standard zero-shot open-vocabulary segmentation benchmarks/datasets, while also being more data-efficient."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025 CDEL Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Haodi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vyom","familyName":"Pathak","name":"Pathak, Vyom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisy Zhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Daisy Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Vision Language Models and Symbolic Grounding for Video Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:35:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Question Answering (VQA) requires models to reason over spatial, temporal, and causal cues in videos. Recent vision language models (VLMs) achieve strong results but often rely on shallow correlations, leading to weak temporal grounding and limited interpretability. We study symbolic scene graphs (SGs) as intermediate grounding signals for VQA. SGs provide structured object-relation representations that complement VLMs holistic reasoning. We introduce SG-VLM, a modular framework that integrates frozen VLMs with scene graph grounding via prompting and visual localization. Across three benchmarks (NExT-QA, iVQA, ActivityNet-QA) and multiple VLMs (QwenVL, InternVL), SG-VLM improves causal and temporal reasoning and outperforms prior baselines, though gains over strong VLMs are limited. These findings highlight both the promise and current limitations of symbolic grounding, and offer guidance for future hybrid VLM-symbolic approaches in video understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:12:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Jamil","name":"Jamil, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kotla Sai","familyName":"Charan","name":"Charan, Kotla Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriparna","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sriparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustava","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Koustava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K J","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, K J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do It Yourself (DIY): Modifying Images for Poems in a Zero-Shot Setting Using Weighted Prompt Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:58:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:21:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Poetry is an expressive form of art that invites multiple interpretations, as readers often bring their own emotions, experiences, and cultural backgrounds into their understanding of a poem. Recognizing this, we aim to generate images for poems and improve these images in a zero-shot setting, enabling audiences to modify images as per their requirements. To achieve this, we introduce a novel Weighted Prompt Manipulation (WPM) technique, which systematically modifies attention weights and text embeddings within diffusion models. By dynamically adjusting the importance of specific words, WPM enhances or suppresses their influence in the final generated image, leading to semantically richer and more contextually accurate visualizations. Our approach exploits diffusion models and large language models (LLMs) such as GPT in conjunction with existing poetry datasets, ensuring a comprehensive and structured methodology for improved image generation in the literary domain. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt at integrating weighted prompt manipulation for enhancing imagery in poetic language."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:13:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonin","familyName":"Sulc","name":"Sulc, Antonin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Hellert","name":"Hellert, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic System with Modal Logic for Autonomous Diagnostics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:03:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T00:10:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The development of intelligent agents, particularly those powered by language models (LMs), has shown a critical role in various environments that require intelligent and autonomous decision-making. Environments are not passive testing grounds, and they represent the data required for agents to learn and exhibit in very challenging conditions that require adaptive, complex, and autonomous capacity to make decisions. While the paradigm of scaling models and datasets has led to remarkable emergent capabilities, we argue that scaling the structure, fidelity, and logical consistency of agent reasoning within these environments is a crucial, yet underexplored, dimension of AI research. This paper introduces a neuro-symbolic multi-agent architecture where the belief states of individual agents are formally represented as Kripke models. This foundational choice enables them to reason about known concepts of \\emph{possibility} and \\emph{necessity} using the formal language of modal logic. In this work, we use immutable, domain-specific knowledge to make an informed root cause diagnosis, which is encoded as logical constraints essential for proper, reliable, and explainable diagnosis. In the proposed model, we show constraints that actively guide the hypothesis generation of LMs, effectively preventing them from reaching physically or logically untenable conclusions. In a high-fidelity simulated particle accelerator environment, our system successfully diagnoses complex, cascading failures by combining the powerful semantic intuition of LMs with the rigorous, verifiable validation of modal logic and a factual world model and showcasing a viable path toward more robust, reliable, and verifiable autonomous agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Youquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyue","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaici","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huaici","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Tsang","familyName":"Ooi","name":"Ooi, Wei Tsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Generate 4D LiDAR Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While generative world models have advanced video and occupancy-based data synthesis, LiDAR generation remains underexplored despite its importance for accurate 3D perception. Extending generation to 4D LiDAR data introduces challenges in controllability, temporal stability, and evaluation. We present LiDARCrafter, a unified framework that converts free-form language into editable LiDAR sequences. Instructions are parsed into ego-centric scene graphs, which a tri-branch diffusion model transforms into object layouts, trajectories, and shapes. A range-image diffusion model generates the initial scan, and an autoregressive module extends it into a temporally coherent sequence. The explicit layout design further supports object-level editing, such as insertion or relocation. To enable fair assessment, we provide EvalSuite, a benchmark spanning scene-, object-, and sequence-level metrics. On nuScenes, LiDARCrafter achieves state-of-the-art fidelity, controllability, and temporal consistency, offering a foundation for LiDAR-based simulation and data augmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Abstract Paper (Non-Archival) @ ICCV 2025 Wild3D Workshop; GitHub Repo at https://lidarcrafter.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huilin","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Huilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec-LLaVA: Accelerating Vision-Language Models with Dynamic Tree-Based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable powerful multimodal reasoning but suffer from slow autoregressive inference, limiting their deployment in real-time applications. We introduce Spec-LLaVA, a system that applies speculative decoding to accelerate VLMs without sacrificing output quality. Spec-LLaVA pairs a lightweight draft VLM with a large target model: the draft speculates future tokens, which the target verifies in parallel, allowing multiple tokens to be generated per step. To maximize efficiency, we design a dynamic tree-based verification algorithm that adaptively expands and prunes speculative branches using draft model confidence. On MS COCO out-of-domain images, Spec-LLaVA achieves up to 3.28$\\times$ faster decoding on LLaVA-1.5 (7B, 13B) with no loss in generation quality. This work presents a lossless acceleration framework for VLMs using dynamic tree-structured speculative decoding, opening a path toward practical real-time multimodal assistants. Importantly, the lightweight draft model design makes the framework amenable to resource-constrained or on-device deployment settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7pages, accepted by ICML TTODLer-FM workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Caiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Vulić","name":"Vulić, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lost in Embeddings: Information Loss in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:28:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision--language models (VLMs) often process visual inputs through a pretrained vision encoder, followed by a projection into the language model's embedding space via a connector component. While crucial for modality fusion, the potential information loss induced by this projection step and its direct impact on model capabilities remain understudied. We introduce two complementary approaches to examine and quantify this loss by analyzing the latent representation space. First, we evaluate semantic information preservation by analyzing changes in k-nearest neighbor relationships between image representations, before and after projection. Second, we directly measure information loss by reconstructing visual embeddings from the projected representation, localizing loss at an image patch level. Experiments reveal that connectors substantially distort the local geometry of visual representations, with k-nearest neighbors diverging by 40--60\\% post-projection, correlating with degradation in retrieval performance. The patch-level embedding reconstruction provides interpretable insights for model behavior on visually grounded question-answering tasks, finding that areas of high information loss reliably predict instances where models struggle."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-driven Smile Design: Personalized Dental Aesthetics Outcomes Using Deep Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.10; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:49:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:29:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A healthy smile plays a significant role in functional as well as esthetic considerations, improving confidence. It is difficult for dental professionals to strike a balance between esthetic requirements and functional requirements. Traditional smile design has had heavy reliance on dentist expertise and used plaster models and hand drawings, raising questions about the outcome for patients. Digital technology, led by Dr. Christian Coachman in 2007, allows photographic and videographic assessments, enabling improved intercommunication among specialists and patients. Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) and big data have supported analysis of facial features and development of personalized smile designs in the last few years. Outputs are, however, susceptible to practitioner bias or limitations of training data, and may be suboptimal for individual users. The study presented here suggests a comprehensive system integrating AI, big data, and recognition technologies to automate the smile design process so that both experienced and inexperienced dentists can generate pleasing aesthetics with ease. The system has a Facial Feature Extraction Module and an Image Generation Module, serving diverse practitioner and patient needs. User data can be incorporated in future research for design optimization and testing of virtual and augmented reality for real-time previewing. Data gathered can also be employed in aesthetic preference analyses, which can enhance our knowledge of smile design in dental practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Finn","familyName":"Carter","name":"Carter, Finn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyue","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Chenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Ku","name":"Ku, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Daheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models: A Theoretical Perspective on Security and Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:05:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T12:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved unprecedented success in image generation but pose increasing risks in terms of privacy, fairness, and security. A growing demand exists to \\emph{erase} sensitive or harmful concepts (e.g., NSFW content, private individuals, artistic styles) from these models while preserving their overall generative capabilities. We introduce \\textbf{SCORE} (Secure and Concept-Oriented Robust Erasure), a novel framework for robust concept removal in diffusion models. SCORE formulates concept erasure as an \\emph{adversarial independence} problem, theoretically guaranteeing that the model's outputs become statistically independent of the erased concept. Unlike prior heuristic methods, SCORE minimizes the mutual information between a target concept and generated outputs, yielding provable erasure guarantees. We provide formal proofs establishing convergence properties and derive upper bounds on residual concept leakage. Empirically, we evaluate SCORE on Stable Diffusion and FLUX across four challenging benchmarks: object erasure, NSFW removal, celebrity face suppression, and artistic style unlearning. SCORE consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods including EraseAnything, ANT, MACE, ESD, and UCE, achieving up to \\textbf{12.5\\%} higher erasure efficacy while maintaining comparable or superior image quality. By integrating adversarial optimization, trajectory consistency, and saliency-driven fine-tuning, SCORE sets a new standard for secure and robust concept erasure in diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"updated version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:25:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Efficient Open-Vocabulary Segmentation in the Remote Sensing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:24:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:31:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T14:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:37:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T06:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-Vocabulary Remote Sensing Image Segmentation (OVRSIS), an emerging task that adapts Open-Vocabulary Segmentation (OVS) to the remote sensing (RS) domain, remains underexplored due to the absence of a unified evaluation benchmark and the domain gap between natural and RS images. To bridge these gaps, we first establish a standardized OVRSIS benchmark (\\textbf{OVRSISBench}) based on widely-used RS segmentation datasets, enabling consistent evaluation across methods. Using this benchmark, we comprehensively evaluate several representative OVS/OVRSIS models and reveal their limitations when directly applied to remote sensing scenarios. Building on these insights, we propose \\textbf{RSKT-Seg}, a novel open-vocabulary segmentation framework tailored for remote sensing. RSKT-Seg integrates three key components: (1) a Multi-Directional Cost Map Aggregation (RS-CMA) module that captures rotation-invariant visual cues by computing vision-language cosine similarities across multiple directions; (2) an Efficient Cost Map Fusion (RS-Fusion) transformer, which jointly models spatial and semantic dependencies with a lightweight dimensionality reduction strategy; and (3) a Remote Sensing Knowledge Transfer (RS-Transfer) module that injects pre-trained knowledge and facilitates domain adaptation via enhanced upsampling. Extensive experiments on the benchmark show that RSKT-Seg consistently outperforms strong OVS baselines by +3.8 mIoU and +5.9 mACC, while achieving 2x faster inference through efficient aggregation. Our code is \\href{https://github.com/LiBingyu01/RSKT-Seg}{\\textcolor{blue}{here}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Isobe","name":"Isobe, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baolu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qinghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiwen","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Haiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huchuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Layout-Conditioned Autoregressive Text-to-Image Generation via Structured Masking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:27:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T12:19:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:54:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-04T12:10:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although autoregressive (AR) models have demonstrated remarkable success in image generation, extending these models to layout-conditioned generation remains challenging due to the sparse nature of layout conditions and the risk of feature entanglement. We present \\textbf{S}tructured \\textbf{M}asking for \\textbf{AR}-based \\textbf{L}ayout-to-\\textbf{I}mage (SMARLI), a novel framework that effectively integrates spatial layout constraints into the AR generation process. To equip AR models with layout control, a structured masking strategy is applied to the attention computation to govern the interaction among the global prompt, layout, and image tokens. This design prevents the misassociation of different regions with their corresponding descriptions while enabling the sufficient injection of layout constraints into the generation process. To alleviate the exposure bias of AR models and further enhance generation quality and layout accuracy, we incorporate a Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) post-training scheme. We adapt it to the next-set-based paradigm and introduce a specifically designed layout reward, which is coordinated with an image quality reward to guide policy optimization in a balanced manner. Experimental results demonstrate that SMARLI seamlessly integrates layout tokens with text and image tokens without compromising generation quality, and the proposed masking strategy and post-training scheme can also be transferred to standard next-token-based AR models. The proposed framework achieves superior layout control while maintaining the structural simplicity and generation efficiency of AR models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ECCV 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhekai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LEGO: Spatial Accelerator Generation and Optimization for Tensor Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:36:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/hpca61900.2025.00101","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern tensor applications, especially foundation models and generative AI applications require multiple input modalities (both vision and language), which increases the demand for flexible accelerator architecture. Existing frameworks suffer from the trade-off between design flexibility and productivity of RTL generation: either limited to very few hand-written templates or cannot automatically generate the RTL. To address this challenge, we propose the LEGO framework, which targets tensor applications and automatically generates spatial architecture design and outputs synthesizable RTL code without handwritten RTL design templates. Leveraging the affine-transformation-based architecture representation, LEGO front end finds interconnections between function units, synthesizes the memory system, and fuses different spatial dataflow designs based on data reuse analysis. LEGO back end then translates the hardware in a primitive-level graph to perform lower-level optimizations, and applies a set of linear-programming algorithms to optimally insert pipeline registers and reduce the overhead of unused logic when switching spatial dataflows. Our evaluation demonstrates that LEGO can achieve 3.2x speedup and 2.4x energy efficiency compared to previous work Gemmini, and can generate one architecture for diverse modern foundation models in generative AI applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The first two authors have equal contributions; Published as a conference paper in HPCA 2025; 13 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Gengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuze","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Embodied Navigation Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T18:15:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:03:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Navigation is a fundamental capability in embodied AI, representing the intelligence required to perceive and interact within physical environments following language instructions. Despite significant progress in large Vision-Language Models (VLMs), which exhibit remarkable zero-shot performance on general vision-language tasks, their generalization ability in embodied navigation remains largely confined to narrow task settings and embodiment-specific architectures. In this work, we introduce a cross-embodiment and cross-task Navigation Foundation Model (NavFoM), trained on eight million navigation samples that encompass quadrupeds, drones, wheeled robots, and vehicles, and spanning diverse tasks such as vision-and-language navigation, object searching, target tracking, and autonomous driving. NavFoM employs a unified architecture that processes multimodal navigation inputs from varying camera configurations and navigation horizons. To accommodate diverse camera setups and temporal horizons, NavFoM incorporates identifier tokens that embed camera view information of embodiments and the temporal context of tasks. Furthermore, to meet the demands of real-world deployment, NavFoM controls all observation tokens using a dynamically adjusted sampling strategy under a limited token length budget. Extensive evaluations on public benchmarks demonstrate that our model achieves state-of-the-art or highly competitive performance across multiple navigation tasks and embodiments without requiring task-specific fine-tuning. Additional real-world experiments further confirm the strong generalization capability and practical applicability of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://pku-epic.github.io/NavFoM-Web/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:36:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look Again, Think Slowly: Enhancing Visual Reflection in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:57:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in text-only \"slow-thinking\" reasoning have prompted efforts to transfer this capability to vision-language models (VLMs), for training visual reasoning models (\\textbf{VRMs}). owever, such transfer faces critical challenges: Effective \"slow thinking\" in VRMs requires \\textbf{visual reflection}, the ability to check the reasoning process based on visual information. Through quantitative analysis, we observe that current VRMs exhibit limited visual reflection, as their attention to visual information diminishes rapidly with longer generated responses. To address this challenge, we propose a new VRM \\textbf{Reflection-V}, which enhances visual reflection based on reasoning data construction for cold-start and reward design for reinforcement learning (RL). Firstly, we construct vision-centered reasoning data by leveraging an agent that interacts between VLMs and reasoning LLMs, enabling cold-start learning of visual reflection patterns. Secondly, a visual attention based reward model is employed during RL to encourage reasoning based on visual information. Therefore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} demonstrates significant improvements across multiple visual reasoning benchmarks. Furthermore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} maintains a stronger and more consistent reliance on visual information during visual reasoning, indicating effective enhancement in visual reflection capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyolim","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hyolim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsu","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yunsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngbeom","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Youngbeom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeeun","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yeeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seon Joo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seon Joo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:11:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:38:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding, a task that combines online temporal action localization with free-form description generation. Given the scarcity of datasets with hierarchical and fine-grained temporal annotations, we demonstrate that LLMs can effectively group atomic actions into higher-level events, enriching existing datasets. We then propose OpenHOUSE (Open-ended Hierarchical Online Understanding System for Events), which extends streaming action perception beyond action classification. OpenHOUSE features a specialized streaming module that accurately detects boundaries between closely adjacent actions, nearly doubling the performance of direct extensions of existing methods. We envision the future of streaming action perception in the integration of powerful generative models, with OpenHOUSE representing a key step in that direction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:20:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12201","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12201","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12201","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianjun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jianjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzheng","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Wenzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zizun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zizun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Kaijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingning","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dingning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhoujie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zhoujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangmiao","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiangmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaipeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniWorld: A Multi-Domain and Multi-Modal Dataset for 4D World Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:42:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T13:15:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of 4D world modeling - aiming to jointly capture spatial geometry and temporal dynamics - has witnessed remarkable progress in recent years, driven by advances in large-scale generative models and multimodal learning. However, the development of truly general 4D world models remains fundamentally constrained by the availability of high-quality data. Existing datasets and benchmarks often lack the dynamic complexity, multi-domain diversity, and spatial-temporal annotations required to support key tasks such as 4D geometric reconstruction, future prediction, and camera-control video generation. To address this gap, we introduce OmniWorld, a large-scale, multi-domain, multi-modal dataset specifically designed for 4D world modeling. OmniWorld consists of a newly collected OmniWorld-Game dataset and several curated public datasets spanning diverse domains. Compared with existing synthetic datasets, OmniWorld-Game provides richer modality coverage, larger scale, and more realistic dynamic interactions. Based on this dataset, we establish a challenging benchmark that exposes the limitations of current state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches in modeling complex 4D environments. Moreover, fine-tuning existing SOTA methods on OmniWorld leads to significant performance gains across 4D reconstruction and video generation tasks, strongly validating OmniWorld as a powerful resource for training and evaluation. We envision OmniWorld as a catalyst for accelerating the development of general-purpose 4D world models, ultimately advancing machines' holistic understanding of the physical world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://yangzhou24.github.io/OmniWorld/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12201","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:27:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":49,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bsize%5D=1000\u0026sort=name\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22video+generation%22+OR+%22image+generation%22+OR+%22multimodal+foundation%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22video+generation%22+OR+%22image+generation%22+OR+%22multimodal+foundation%22%29%29"}}